#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="UserPromptSubmit hook body: release every live hold, so the sleeper exits on the human's reply instead of being killed by it"
#
# CLOUD-451. The other half of `plan-hold`. A human answering is the event that
# ends the wait, and `UserPromptSubmit` is where the harness announces it —
# already wired, once per prompt rather than once per tool call, so this costs
# nothing in the class CLOUD-435 removed.
#
# **Release, not kill.** Removing the sentinel lets the hold finish on its own
# terms: it returns 0, and its completion is a wake-up the harness delivers with
# the human's message already in hand. Signalling it instead would end the
# process without that exit, which is the same "the work runs and the session
# loses its wake-up" failure `run-shape-guard` catalogues — and this file sends
# no signal at all, so the property holds structurally rather than by review.
#
# **NOT EVERY PROMPT IS A PERSON, and the first version of this file assumed
# otherwise.** Measured on a session container: a hold was armed, a background
# task finished eight seconds later, and its completion notification arrived as a
# `UserPromptSubmit` — the hold reported "released after 15s" with nobody having
# typed anything. Webhook deliveries land the same way. That is not a cosmetic
# miss: dropping the door while the human is mid-sentence is the exact failure
# CLOUD-451 exists to prevent, reintroduced by the half of the mechanism meant to
# clean up after it.
#
# So the predicate is "a human submitted this", and it is decided by the
# envelope: machine turns arrive wrapped — `<task-notification>`,
# `<github-webhook-activity>`, `<system-reminder>`, `<untrusted_external_data>` —
# or opened with a bracketed `[SYSTEM …` banner. Anything tag-shaped or
# banner-shaped is not a person.
#
# **The bias is deliberate and one-directional.** Holding when it should have
# released costs a sleeping process that its own cap will end, and the guard is
# idempotent so nothing double-arms. Releasing when it should have held costs the
# human their typed input. Those are not comparable, so an unrecognised shape
# holds. Only the prompt's opening bytes are ever examined, and nothing is
# emitted (non-negotiable 4) — the classification is a shape, never a read of
# what somebody wrote.
#
# Releasing EVERY hold, not one keyed to this session: two sessions sharing a
# clone is already discouraged, the worst case is that one of them stops waiting
# a little early, and the alternative is threading a session id through a task
# environment that does not reliably carry one.
#
# Silent and always 0. A hook on the prompt path has no verdict to deliver and
# nothing worth saying — an agent does not need to be told that a sentinel it
# never sees was removed.
#
# Incidentally measured by the same probe, and worth knowing because
# `contract-drift` warns the other way: a `UserPromptSubmit` entry added
# mid-session DID fire in that session. Whatever the harness re-reads, it is not
# only `SessionStart`.
set -uo pipefail

raw=$(cat 2>/dev/null) || exit 0

check="$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/plan-hold-release-check"
[ -x "$check" ] || exit 0
printf '%s' "$raw" | "$check" || exit 0

check="$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/plan-hold-check"
[ -x "$check" ] || exit 0

dir=$("$check" dir 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$dir" ] && [ -d "$dir" ] || exit 0

for file in "$dir"/*; do
	[ -f "$file" ] || continue
	rm -f -- "$file" 2>/dev/null || true
done

exit 0
